# ctrlisync.map is the REJECTED ctrl+isync acquire-load proposal (POWER-STRENGTHS.md item
# 10): keep LDAR's leading hwsync, but replace the TRAILING lwsync on LDAR and LDAPR with
# `cmpw %r,%r; bne lbl; lbl: isync` -- the self-compare, never-taken branch and isync that
# GCC's rs6000 backend and LLVM's PPC backend both emit for a C++ acquire load.
#
# This is NOT trailing.map. The store side does not move, LDAR keeps its leading hwsync, and
# SC-IRIW1 -- the all-seq_cst IRIW that killed trailing-sync -- stays FORBIDDEN here. The
# proposal fails for a different and previously unrecorded reason.
#
#   current    1052 translated,  6 UNSOUND   (all six listed in current.known)
#   ctrlisync  1052 translated, 10 UNSOUND   (those six, plus the four below)
#
#     IRWIW008  RfePA PodRWAP Coe RfePA PodRWAP Coe
#     IRWIW012  RfePQ PodRWQP Coe RfePQ PodRWQP Coe
#     WWC012    RfePA PodRWAP RfePA PodRWAP Coe
#     WWC018    RfePQ PodRWQP RfePQ PodRWQP Coe
#
# WHY. All four are read->write: an acquire load, then a later plain store, with the cycle
# closed by coherence. The acquire load must not merely be ordered before that store on its
# own thread -- the write it READ FROM must also propagate before that store. That is
# A-cumulativity, and it is the one thing ctrl+isync does not have.
#
# It is not a missing local edge. ctrl+isync does order read->write on the executing thread:
# LB with ctrl+isync on both threads is Never, LB with plain program order is Sometimes
# (diyone7 -arch PPC, `Rfe DpCtrlIsyncdW` twice). ppc.cat puts ctrlisync in `ci0` only, so it
# reaches `ppo` and `hb` and stops there, while ppc-checks.cat's
#
#     let fence = strong|light           (* sync | lwsync | eieio *)
#     let propbase = (fence|(rfe;fence));hbstar
#     let prop = propbase & (W * W) | (chapo? ; propbase*; strong; hbstar)
#     acyclic co|prop as propagation
#
# can only build a W*W propagation edge out of a FENCE. `rfe;fence` is the cumulative term,
# and ctrl+isync is not a fence, so there is no edge to build. lwsync gives the same local
# read->write ordering AND that term, which is the entire difference between the two columns.
# The leading hwsync cannot stand in for it: it is po-BEFORE the load, so `rfe;fence` never
# matches it.
#
# Nor is a bare isync a cheaper route to the same place: 35f48f28b recorded that the
# pre-147903303 `lwsync; isync` lowering had exactly current's six divergences, because isync
# without a control dependency adds no ordering in the Power model at all. Confirmed again
# here (`ISyncdRR`: MP Sometimes; `DpCtrlIsyncdR`: MP Never).
#
# WHY TWO COMPILERS STILL CHOOSE IT. GCC and LLVM are compiling C and C++, where an acquire
# load only has to synchronize with a RELEASE store, so the cumulativity it needs arrives
# through the release side. AArch64's LDAR/LDAPR promise more than C++ acquire: the model is
# other-multi-copy-atomic, so a write an LDAR has read is visible to everyone, and every
# later access is ordered after it whether the writer released or not. An emulator owes the
# guest the architecture, not the source language. ctrl+isync is sound for the contract the
# two compilers are keeping and unsound for the one POWERarm has to keep.
#
# FOR THE RECORD, because it is the first question anyone will ask: all four ARE outside what
# a C or C++ program can rely on, which is the test current.known applies to its six. Both
# shapes transcribed into C with every plain access a relaxed atomic are Sometimes under
# rc11.cat. So the four could be argued into a .known file. They are not listed below, for
# two reasons. The six in current.known are read->read multi-copy-atomicity, reachable only
# by hand-written assembly relying on it; these four give up a cumulative fence's propagation
# in the read->write direction, which is the ordering a JIT-emitted guest (V8, JSC) gets from
# LDAR without any release store in sight. And accepting them would be deciding that
# POWERarm's acquire load implements C++ acquire rather than AArch64 LDAR -- a policy change,
# on by default for 126,727 sites in libxul, which is the owner's call and not a map file's.
#
# Nothing is listed below on purpose, so `check.sh ctrlisync` reports every divergence and
# exits 1. Kept, like trailing.map, as the record of why the acquire load keeps its trailing
# lwsync. No backend code was written for this proposal.
